{"reference":"Job 17","book":"Job","chapter":17,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"In the Wider Dialogue","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Job 17 belongs to the extended debate that follows the friends’ seven days of silent presence. In that debate, Eliphaz, Bildad, and Zophar increasingly explain Job’s suffering by assuming wrongdoing, while Job rejects that diagnosis. This wider setting helps explain the chapter’s sharp references to mockers, provocation, and the friends’ lack of wisdom.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_017_b934c99988bfb8a856906d2a"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"The statement that the righteous will hold to their way and grow stronger (vv. 8–9) has sometimes been connected with Job’s endurance across the whole story. In this reading, steadfastness means persistence through suffering and the eventual outcome of the story; it should not be taken to require silent endurance of abuse or to forbid seeking protection.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_017_fd75831afaf96400f658d3b6"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}